.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt STTY 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm stty
.Nd set the options of a terminal
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl a | e | g
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Ar operand ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
shows or changes the settings of a terminal: the standard input, or
.Ar file .
With no operand it writes the speed, then the flags that differ from their
defaults, grouped as local
.Pq Ql lflags ,
input
.Pq Ql iflags ,
output
.Pq Ql oflags
and control
.Pq Ql cflags ,
and a table of the control characters that differ from theirs.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Write every setting, and the window size, the control characters as
.Ql name = value; .
.It Fl e
Write every setting, and the window size, the control characters as a table.
.It Fl f Ar file
Use the terminal
.Ar file
rather than the standard input.
.It Fl g
Write every setting on one line that, given back to
.Nm
as an operand, restores them all:
.Dl $ saved=$(stty -g); stty raw; ...; stty \&"$saved\&"
.El
.Pp
Operands change settings, all of them at once when they are read.
A flag is turned on by its name and off by its name after a
.Ql - .
.Ss Control flags
.Bl -tag -width "-crtscts" -compact
.It Cm cs5 cs6 cs7 cs8
Character size in bits.
.It Cm cstopb
Two stop bits rather than one.
.It Cm cread
Enable the receiver.
.It Cm parenb
Generate and check parity.
.It Cm parodd
Odd parity rather than even.
.It Cm hupcl
Hang up when the last process closes the terminal;
.Cm hup
is the same.
.It Cm clocal
No modem control lines.
.It Cm crtscts
RTS/CTS flow control.
.El
.Ss Input flags
.Bl -tag -width "-crtscts" -compact
.It Cm ignbrk
Ignore a break.
.It Cm brkint
A break interrupts (sends
.Dv SIGINT ) .
.It Cm ignpar
Ignore characters with parity errors.
.It Cm parmrk
Mark parity errors.
.It Cm inpck
Check input parity.
.It Cm istrip
Strip input to seven bits.
.It Cm inlcr
Map newline to carriage return.
.It Cm igncr
Ignore carriage return.
.It Cm icrnl
Map carriage return to newline.
.It Cm ixon
Start and stop output with the
.Cm start
and
.Cm stop
characters.
.It Cm ixoff
Send
.Cm start
and
.Cm stop
when the input queue fills and drains;
.Cm tandem
is the same.
.It Cm ixany
Any character restarts output;
.Cm decctlq
is the opposite.
.It Cm imaxbel
Ring the bell when the input queue is full.
.El
.Ss Output flags
.Bl -tag -width "-crtscts" -compact
.It Cm opost
Process output.
.It Cm onlcr
Map newline to carriage return and newline.
.It Cm ocrnl
Map carriage return to newline.
.It Cm onocr
Don't write a carriage return at column 0.
.It Cm onlret
Newline does a carriage return.
.It Cm oxtabs
Expand tabs to spaces;
.Cm tabs
is
.Cm -oxtabs .
.El
.Ss Local flags
.Bl -tag -width "-crtscts" -compact
.It Cm isig
Generate signals for
.Cm intr ,
.Cm quit
and
.Cm susp .
.It Cm icanon
Line by line input, with editing.
.It Cm iexten
Extended input processing
.Pq Cm lnext , werase , reprint , discard .
.It Cm echo
Echo input.
.It Cm echoe
Erase a character on the screen for
.Cm erase ;
.Cm crterase
is the same.
.It Cm echok
Echo a newline after
.Cm kill .
.It Cm echoke
Erase the line on the screen for
.Cm kill ;
.Cm crtkill
is the same.
.It Cm echonl
Echo newline even without
.Cm echo .
.It Cm echoctl
Echo control characters as
.Ql ^X ;
.Cm ctlecho
is the same.
.It Cm echoprt
Echo erased characters between
.Ql \e
and
.Ql / ;
.Cm prterase
is the same.
.It Cm noflsh
Don't flush the queues on
.Cm intr ,
.Cm quit
or
.Cm susp .
.It Cm tostop
Stop background jobs that write to the terminal.
.It Cm flusho
Output is being discarded.
.It Cm pendin
Input is to be retyped.
.El
.Ss Control characters
Each takes a value: a character, as itself or as
.Ql ^X
.Pq Ql ^?\&
for DEL), or
.Cm undef
or
.Ql ^-
to disable it.
.Bl -tag -width "-crtscts" -compact
.It Cm discard
Discard output (also
.Cm flush ) .
.It Cm eof
End of file.
.It Cm eol , eol2
Extra end of line characters
.Pq Cm brk No is Cm eol .
.It Cm erase
Erase a character.
.It Cm intr
Interrupt.
.It Cm kill
Erase the line.
.It Cm lnext
Take the next character literally.
.It Cm quit
Quit.
.It Cm reprint
Retype the line (also
.Cm rprnt ) .
.It Cm start , stop
Restart and stop output.
.It Cm susp
Suspend.
.It Cm werase
Erase a word.
.It Cm min , time
For
.Cm -icanon :
the fewest characters a read waits for, as a number, and how long, in tenths
of a second.
.El
.Ss Combinations and others
.Bl -tag -width "-crtscts" -compact
.It Ar number
Set both speeds, in baud.
.It Cm ispeed Ar n , Cm ospeed Ar n
Set the input or output speed.
.It Cm speed
Write the speed.
.It Cm rows Ar n , Cm cols Ar n
Set the window size
.Cm ( columns
is
.Cm cols ) .
.It Cm size
Write the window size, rows then columns.
.It Cm all , everything
Write the settings as
.Fl e
and
.Fl a
do.
.It Cm sane
Reset the flags to reasonable values, keeping the speed,
.Cm clocal
and the echo style.
.Cm cooked ,
.Cm -raw
and
.Cm -cbreak
are the same.
.It Cm raw
No input or output processing: characters are passed as they come.
.It Cm cbreak
Characters as they come, but with signals and output processing.
.It Cm evenp , parity
Even parity, seven bits;
.Cm oddp
odd parity; with a
.Ql -
any of them is no parity, eight bits.
.It Cm pass8
Eight bits, no parity;
.Cm -pass8
seven bits with parity.
.It Cm litout
Eight bits, no parity, no output processing;
.Cm -litout
the reverse.
.It Cm crt , newcrt
.Cm echoe echoke echoctl ;
with a
.Ql -
.Cm echok
instead.
.It Cm dec
.Cm crt ,
.Cm -ixany ,
erase
.Ql ^? ,
kill
.Ql ^U
and intr
.Ql ^C .
.It Cm ek
Erase and kill back to
.Ql ^?
and
.Ql ^U .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Read a password without echoing it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
stty -echo; read pw; stty echo
.Ed
.Pp
Show the settings of another terminal:
.Dl # stty -a -f /dev/ttyv1
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tcsetattr 3 ,
.Xr pts 4 ,
.Xr termios 4
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq),
less the XSI output delay settings
.Pq Cm ofill , ofdel , cr0 No ... Cm ff1 .
.Fl e ,
.Fl f
and the combinations not in POSIX are extensions, as in the BSDs.
The BSDs'
.Cm altwerase ,
.Cm dsusp ,
.Cm erase2 ,
.Cm status
and line discipline settings are not provided: OxideBSD's terminals don't
have them.
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v2 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
